# Defensive security audit

Use the repository, subsystem or diff supplied by the caller. For an ordinary review, limit the assessment to changed code and its affected control paths. Use source evidence or a local, side-effect-free observation of expected control behaviour with benign inputs. Keep any local observation sequential. Do not use external targets, run live exploits, write project files, or run a test suite, fixer, analyser or build.

1. Record the local revision, read-only snapshot, permitted scope and selected profile. Compute the budget with `skills/security-audit/scripts/audit-artifacts.sh budget`. Preserve the verification and coverage reserves. A valid previous run may supply unchanged evidence under `@skills/security-audit/references/incremental-audit.md`.
2. Build the coverage ledger before opening any assessment. Enumerate the access policies, validation, isolation and data-handling controls in scope. Record excluded surfaces and their reasons. Validate the inventory with `skills/security-audit/scripts/audit-artifacts.sh inventory`.
3. Assign one documented coverage unit per static assessment and use the bounded contract in `@skills/security-audit/SKILL.md`. Parallel passes are permitted only if all four conditions hold. Each fresh worker takes one disjoint coverage unit, receives only its brief, and returns its result in its reply. It writes no files anywhere. No unit in the wave needs a local reproduction or other runtime observation. The host enforces reading-only tools: on Claude select a fresh Task with only Read, Grep and Glob, and on Codex use a fresh subagent in a read-only sandbox. If that enforcement is unavailable, run one unit at a time in the current context. Diff mode always uses sequential passes. Only the coordinator writes the designated audit artifacts after the wave. Snapshot before and after every wave; on a difference end parallel assessment and report the write. A snapshot covers HEAD, the index and tracked or non-ignored files. It cannot detect a write to an ignored path, outside the working tree, or within a submodule or nested repository, so it cannot establish read-only execution.
4. Deduplicate results by their deterministic fingerprint. Give each candidate to a fresh read-only verifier. The verifier must have proposed none of the candidates and verifies at most one. Confirm or reject the candidate from the documented controls and source evidence. Use `needs_validation` where runtime evidence would be required.
5. A fresh coverage assessment checks each wave against the original inventory, records gaps, then returns any uncovered in-scope units for another bounded pass. Preserve the budget reserves. Record deferred units when capacity is exhausted. A missing independent verification or an unassessed unit remains visible in the report.
6. Seal each unit with its paths, input digest and verdict. Write the existing coverage, findings and report artifacts, recheck the snapshot, and run the existing deterministic validator. Apply the skill's Diff-mode severity and disclosure rules when returning a PR review result. Report any failed validation as incomplete.

A parallel wave requires a remaining coverage-critic pass before it opens. The coordinator allocates the original hunting, verification and critic reserves from the selected profile. Reused evidence spends no pass. Finish deferred units with a stated reason and compare the final ledger to the original inventory.
